// adversarial-target: coordinated-source-to-core-false-claim
//
// D-05-30's adversarial demonstration (plan 05-13). This SOURCE program is
// an ordinary, checker-accepted, natively-executable ownership transfer:
// `relay` takes full ownership of its buffer parameter and hands it
// straight back to the caller (the exact shape of testdata/phase2/
// owned_transfer.schway). Read alone, both by a human and by `check` plus
// `originvalidate`, this program honestly claims: the caller's buffer is
// MOVED into the function and returned, a complete ownership transfer.
//
// The paired testdata/phase5/coordinated_lie.core.json is a HAND-AUTHORED
// core artifact naming this exact module/function identity
// (phase5.coordinated_lie / relay) that instead claims the returned Buffer
// is only ever SHARED-BORROWED -- never moved, never fully transferred.
// That is a different, false ownership-transfer claim about the same named
// function. Both artifacts independently pass their own validator (this
// source passes `check` and `originvalidate.ValidatePublished`; the core
// JSON independently passes `corevalidate.Validate` on its own terms) and
// neither validator ever compares one artifact against the other --
// exactly the boundary corevalidate.KnownEscape /
// originvalidate.KnownEscape both document. Demonstrating this escape is
// not closing it: no control in this repository closes a coordinated
// source-to-core lie.

module phase5.coordinated_lie

export {
  fn relay
}

fn relay(buffer: Buffer) -> Buffer {
  let delivered = take buffer
  delivered
}
